e great","H3381":"to go down","H1661":"Gath","H6430":"Philistine","H2896":"pleasant","H4480":"from","H4467":"kingdom","H428":"these","H518":"if","H7227":"many","H1366":"boundary","H5077":"to drive away","H3117":"day","H7451":"harmful","H5066":"approach","H7675":"seat","H2555":"violence","H7901":"lay down","H5921":"upon","H4296":"bed","H8127":"tooth","H5628":"to overrun","H6210":"bed","H398":"to eat","H3733":"saddle","H6629":"flock","H5695":"calf","H8432":"midst","H4770":"stall","H6527":"to divide","H6310":"lip","H5035":"bag","H1732":"David","H2803":"design","H3627":"article/utensil","H7892":"song","H8354":"to drink","H4219":"bowl","H3196":"wine","H8081":"oil","H4886":"to anoint","H3808":"not","H2470":"weak","H7667":"breaking","H3130":"Joseph","H3651":"right","H6258":"now","H1540":"Heglam","H7218":"head","H5493":"remove","H4797":"banquet","H7650":"to swear","H136":"Lord","H3069":"YHWH/God","H5315":"soul","H5002":"utterance","H3068":"LORD","H430":"God","H6635":"army","H8374":"to loathe","H595":"I","H853":"[Obj.]","H1347":"pride","H3290":"Jacob","H759":"palace","H8130":"to hate","H5462":"to shut","H5892":"excitement","H4393":"fullness","H1961":"to be","H3498":"to remain","H6235":"ten","H376":"man","H259":"one","H4191":"to die","H5375":"raise","H1730":"beloved","H5635":"to burn","H3318":"come","H6106":"bone","H559":"to say","H834":"which","H3411":"flank","H5750":"still","H5973":"with","H657":"end","H2013":"to silence","H3588":"for","H2142":"to remember","H8034":"name","H2009":"behold","H6680":"to command","H5221":"to smite","H1419":"large","H7447":"drop","H6996":"small","H1233":"breach","H7323":"run","H5553":"rock","H5483":"swallow","H2790":"to plow/plot","H1241":"cattle","H2015":"to overturn","H7219":"poison","H4941":"judgement","H6529":"fruit","H6666":"righteousness","H3939":"wormwood","H8055":"to rejoice","H1697":"word","H2392":"strength","H3947":"take","H7161":"horn","H2005":"look!","H6965":"(Leb)-kamai","H3905":"to oppress","H2574":"Hamath","H5704":"till","H5158":"river","H6160":"Arabah"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["haydock",1,null,"haydock","Wealthy. Septuagint, Syriac, and Arabic, “despisers of Sion.” Hebrew also, “who hate Sion.” The prophecy wholly regards Israel. (Calmet) — It is a great crime for the rich to neglect the poor; but still more so, when wealthy clergymen shew no compassion for the spiritual or corporal wants of their neighbours. (Worthington) — State. Hebrew, “to whom the house of Israel comes” for judgment."],["haydock",2,null,"haydock","Chalane. Ctesiphon (Calmet) was built on its ruins, Genesis x. 10. (Haydock) — Why do you imitate these cities? or, has their greatness protected them? Phul probably took Chalane, and Jeroboam II the other cities, ver. 15., and 4 Kings xiv. 25. At that time there was no appearance of the kingdom being destroyed; yet Amos composes a funeral canticle, to shew the certainty of the event."],["haydock",3,null,"haydock","Separated. Hebrew, “remove the evil day,” as if it would not overtake you, Ezechiel xii. 22. Septuagint, “who are praying (Calmet) or coming (Grabe) to the evil day, approaching and touching false sabbaths.” (Haydock) — They pray to be delivered, while they continue (Calmet) their false worship. (Haydock)"],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","Ivory, with which the beds for eating were adorned, ver. 7. (Calmet) — Wanton. Hebrew, “stretch themselves out upon their,” &c. (Haydock)"],["haydock",5,null,"haydock","David. They think they excel him in music; but he consecrated his talent to a better purpose. (Calmet) — Septuagint, “they deemed them stable, and not fugitive things.” (Haydock) — They have placed their chief good in such pleasures. (Theodoret) (Calmet)"],["haydock",6,null,"haydock","In bowels. Septuagint, “refined,” (Haydock) or cleared of the dregs. — Joseph, of their brethren, or they seem to have no share in the sufferings of mankind, Psalm lxxii. 5."],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","Luxurious. Hebrew, “the feast of those who stretch themselves out, shall,” &c. Septuagint, “the neighing shall be removed from Ephraim.” His lusts shall be punished, Jeremias v. 8. — Some translate [the] Hebrew, “the mourning of those who stretch themselves on their beds is at hand.” Others, “their funeral feast is distant.” None shall bewail their death. So ambiguous is the original. (Calmet)"],["haydock",8,null,"haydock","Jacob. God loved the humility of the patriarch, and hated the pride of his posterity. (Worthington)"],["haydock",9,null,"haydock","Die. Their numbers will not protect them from the plague."],["haydock",10,null,"haydock","Burn. After the captivity, it was more common to bury or to embalm the dead. (Calmet)"],["haydock",11,null,"jerome","Lord. He has done it. Do not repine. (Theodoret; St. Cyril) — Hebrew, “Be silent, and not to remember the,” &c. He will offer comfort. (Calmet) — Still, none will return to the Lord. (St. Jerome)"],["haydock",12,null,"haydock","Clefts. All shall perish, (Calmet) both Israel and Juda. (Chaldean) (Grotius) — But he speaks only of the former."],["haydock",13,null,"haydock","Buffles, which cannot be tamed. Hebrew, “with oxen.” We must understand, on rocks. (Calmet) — Septuagint, “Shall they be silent when they are with females?” (Haydock) — To turn the works of justice into sins, is no less unnatural than to plough with wild buffles. (Worthington)"],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","Naught: in your idols, which are nothing, (1 Corinthians viii. 4.) or in your own strength, fortifications, or allies. — Horns: glory and power. (Calmet) — Parata tollo cornua. (Horace, epod. 6.)"],["haydock",15,null,"haydock","Nation; the Assyrians. — Desert, commonly called Bezor, (Calmet) between Damietta and Rhinocorura. (St. Jerome) — The whole territory of Israel, reaching so far, (ver. 2.) shall be laid waste. (Calmet)"]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"],"jerome":["Jerome",1,1,420,"https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/#jerome"]},"fa":[[1,null,0,"The Five Books Against Marcion","Book 4",0,220,"Jesus' miracle was performed in the district of Samaria, to which country also belonged one of the lepers. Samaria, however, had revolted from Israel, carrying with it the disaffected nine tribes, which having been alienated by the prophet Ahijah, Jeroboam settled in Samaria. Besides, the Samaritans were always pleased with the mountains and the wells of their ancestors. Thus, in the Gospel of John, the woman of Samaria, when conversing with the Lord at the well, says, \"No doubt yours are greater,\" and again, \"Our fathers worshiped in this mountain, but you say that Jerusalem is the place where men ought to worship. …"]],"fau":[["tertullian","Tertullian",0,"ecclesiastical-writer",null,0]],"ftr":[["Ante-Nicene Fathers, vol. 3 (1885)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/anf03"]]}